<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">triggers</ansi>

A <ansi fg="command">trigger</ansi> watches incoming game text and runs one or
more commands automatically when a line matches — with
an optional condition to decide what to do.

<ansi fg="yellow">How it works:</ansi>

  When a line of text arrives from the game, each enabled
  trigger checks whether the line matches its pattern. If
  it does (and any condition is satisfied), the trigger
  fires its commands — hands-free.

  Triggers run only in the web client. Definitions save
  to your account and sync across sessions.

<ansi fg="yellow">Managing triggers:</ansi>

  Triggers are created and managed in the
  <ansi fg="command">Triggers &amp; Timers</ansi> panel — there is no typed
  command for them.

  <ansi fg="command">+ New</ansi>         Open the editor to create a trigger.
  <ansi fg="command">Toggle</ansi>        Enable or disable a trigger without
                 deleting it. Disabled triggers never fire.
  <ansi fg="command">Left-click</ansi>    Test-fire the trigger's commands right now,
                 once.
  <ansi fg="command">Right-click</ansi>   Open the context menu to Edit, Duplicate,
                 or Remove a trigger.

<ansi fg="yellow">Patterns &amp; wildcards:</ansi>

  A pattern is plain text with optional <ansi fg="command">*</ansi> wildcards.
  Each <ansi fg="command">*</ansi> matches any run of characters and captures
  what it matched into <ansi fg="command">$1</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">$2</ansi>, and so on, in the
  order the wildcards appear.

  You can use those captures inside your commands:

    Pattern:  <ansi fg="command">* hits you for * damage.</ansi>
    Command:  <ansi fg="command">say $1 just hit me!</ansi>

  If the line <ansi fg="command">A troll hits you for heavy damage.</ansi>
  matches, then <ansi fg="command">$1</ansi> is <ansi fg="command">A troll</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">$2</ansi> is <ansi fg="command">heavy</ansi>.

  Separate multiple commands with a semicolon:

    <ansi fg="command">cast heal;say Healing up!</ansi>

<ansi fg="yellow">Optional condition (if/else):</ansi>

  Each trigger may have one condition with a Then branch,
  an Else branch, or both. An empty branch means: do
  nothing for that outcome.

  The condition has five sources:

  <ansi fg="command">HP % / SP % / CP %</ansi>
    Check your current usable pool percentage.
    Operators: <ansi fg="command">is below</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">is above</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">equals</ansi>.
    Value: a number from 0 to 100.

    Pool percentages are measured against your
    <ansi fg="command">usable (unreserved)</ansi> pool, not the full pool.
    If half your health is reserved, "HP below 30%"
    triggers at 30% of the remaining half — i.e. when
    your usable health bar is below 30%, not the whole
    bar.

  <ansi fg="command">My conditions</ansi>
    Check whether a status effect is active on you.
    Operators: <ansi fg="command">includes</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">excludes</ansi>.
    Value: a status name (e.g. <ansi fg="command">poisoned</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">bleeding</ansi>).

  <ansi fg="command">A pattern capture</ansi>
    Check the text captured by a wildcard (<ansi fg="command">$1</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">$2</ansi>...).
    Operators: <ansi fg="command">equals</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">contains</ansi>.
    Value: the text to compare against.

  <ansi fg="command">My target</ansi>
    Check the name of your current combat target.
    Operators: <ansi fg="command">is one of</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">is not one of</ansi>.
    Value: a comma-separated list of names. Matches
    any name in the list.

  <ansi fg="command">The ability cooldown</ansi>
    Your special abilities — kick, trip, bash, grapple,
    taunt, rally, warcry, and casting any spell — all share
    one cooldown. This checks whether that shared cooldown
    is free (no value to enter).
    Operators: <ansi fg="command">is ready</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">is not ready</ansi>.
    "Ready" means you are not currently on that cooldown.

<ansi fg="yellow">Cooldown:</ansi>

  After a trigger fires, it waits about one second
  before it can fire again on the same incoming line.
  This prevents a single matching line from looping
  your commands.

<ansi fg="magenta-bold">See also:</ansi> <ansi fg="command">help ticks</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help macros</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help alias</ansi>
